\ DIS8086.SEQ   8086 Disassembler  by Charles Curley    F-PC native port

\ PREFIX  \ Conversion by  Bill Muench  9 September 88  Fixes
\ More 'Not Used' Trapped
\ XCHGA for NOP  FES for 8reg INC DEC
\ REP REPNE  MUL/DIV  POP CS  ESC

comment:

  Native port: there is no 8086 code on the VM. CODE words are abstract
machine code (SRC/AMASM.SEQ), and the disassembler for them lives in
DISASSEM.SEQ, which this file loads. What is left here are the names this
file used to define, so that files which load it still work:

        DIS8086         the vocabulary (now empty; FDIS86.SEQ used it)
        SEE <name>      hooked through (SEE): CODE words show their ops
        SEEN ( cfa -- ) SEE an already ticked word
        DIS ( | name -- )       NOTE: was DIS ( addr -- ); that is ADIS now
        DM ( a -- a' )  display memory, 16 bytes a line, until ESC
        IDIS ( int# -- )        no interrupt vectors on the VM: an error
        #INT2@ ( int# -- seg off )      returns 0 0

  To DisAssemble to a file:
  PFILE <file_spec>
  PRINT SEE <name>
  PCLOSE

comment;

FORTH DEFINITIONS  DECIMAL

NEEDS DISASSEM.SEQ

VOCABULARY DIS8086

: SEEN   ( cfa -- )     \ SEE a word whose CFA is known
        (SEE) ;

: DM-LINE ( a -- a' )   \ one line of DM
        CR DUP 8 U.R ." : "
        16 0 DO DUP I + C@ 3 .R LOOP  2 SPACES
        16 0 DO DUP I + C@ 127 AND DUP 32 127 WITHIN 0=
                IF DROP ASCII _ THEN EMIT
             LOOP  16 + ;

: DM   ( a -- a' )      \ Display Memory
        BEGIN   DM-LINE ?KEYPAUSE
        AGAIN   ;

: #INT2@   ( int# -- seg off )  \ no interrupt vectors on the VM
        DROP 0 0 ;

: IDIS   ( int# -- )
        DROP TRUE ABORT" No interrupt vectors on the VM" ;

CR .( DIS8086 LOADED: the abstract-machine disassembler of DISASSEM.SEQ )
